'''Kuarupí''' ({{IPA|gug|['a.βa.ɲeˈʔẽ]}}), natively called '''''Avañe'ẽ''''' ({{Literal translation|people's speech}}) is a [[Kurupiran-Nyengatú languages|Kurupiran-Nyengatú]] language (of the [[W:Jê–Tupi–Carib languages languages|Korobiri-Kurupiran family]]) spoken by approximately 4.2 million people in [[Balisca]], primarily within the wider [[Concordia]] region, stretching north to [[tba]] and south to [[Brasal]]. It is one of the xx recognized [[Indigenous languages of Balisca|indigenous languages]] of Balisca, and holds the status of a protected regional language under the [[Basic Laws of Balisca]].

The language is known for its [[nasal vowel]]s, [[agglutinative]] morphology, and verb-initial [[word order]], as well as its extensive system of [[evidentiality]] markers.

==Classification==
{{Main|Korobiri-Kurupiran languages}}

Kuarupí is a member of the [[Kurupiran languages|Kurupiran branch]] of the [[Korobiri-Kurupiran languages|Korobiri-Kurupiran language family]]. The [[Kuarupíba]] are one of the [[Kurupiranga|Kurupiranga peoples]], and share a common ancestral origin in the south-to-north [[Pre-colonial Abyala|pre-colonial]] [[Kurupiran expansion|migrations]] from the [[Korobiri highlands]] and around the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], and their languages gradually diverged approximately 800–1,000 years ago.

==History==
{{See also|History of the Kuarupíba people}}

The [[Kuarupíba]] people migrated into the eastern lowlands of Balisca from the [[Korobiri highlands]] between 1000 and 1200 CE, part of the broader [[Kurupiran expansion]] that spread the language family across [[Abyala]]. By the time of [[Volisanian colonization of Balisca|Volisanian contact]] in the early 16th century, the Kuarupíba occupied a vast territory spanning the eastern coastal plains, the fertile river valleys of the interior, and the southern pampas. The Kuarupíba practiced a combination of [[W:Swidden agriculture|swidden agriculture]] (cultivating [[W:Manioc|manioc]], [[W:Maize|maize]], [[W:Sweet potato|sweet potato]], and [[W:Peanuts|peanuts]]), [[W:Fishing|fishing]], and [[W:Hunting|hunting]]. Their social organization was based on [[W:Extended family|extended family]] units organized into larger villages (''oka''), which were frequently relocated as soil fertility declined.

The Volisanian colonizers established the [[Jesuit missions of Balisca]] among the Kuarupíba beginning in the 1580s. The Jesuits developed a writing system for Kuarupí based on the [[W:Latin script|Mesogean script]], produced grammars and dictionaries, and used the language as a [[W:Lingua franca|lingua franca]] for evangelization throughout the eastern territories. This period saw the first substantial written records of the language. However, colonial policies also led to significant population decline among the Kuarupíba due to [[W:Epidemic disease|epidemics]] and [[W:Forced relocation|forced relocation]] due to settler enroachment. The Kuarupíba population declined from an estimated 1.2 million in 1500 to approximately 250,000 by 1700, with the language surviving primarily in isolated inland communities villages.

Following Baliscan independence, the Kuarupíba faced continued pressure from [[W:Agricultural expansion|agricultural expansion]] and [[W:Land enclosure|land enclosure]]. The [[W:Monoculture|monoculture]] of [[coffee]] and [[sugar]] in the eastern lowlands displaced many Kuarupíba communities, while government policies promoted displacement or [[W:Assimilation|assimilation]] into the dominant Baliscano-speaking society. Despite these pressures, the language persisted in rural areas and was maintained by the [[Kuarupíba diaspora]] in urban centers. The late 19th century saw the emergence of a [[Kuarupíba literature|written literature]] in Kuarupí, including newspapers, poetry, and religious texts.

The [[Baliscan Revolution]] of 1918–1920, brought significant changes for indigenous communities and their languages. The [[Processo Revolucionario en Curso|revolutionary government's]] policy of [[W:Cultural pluralism|cultural pluralism]] and [[W:Indigenous rights|indigenous rights]] led to the establishment of [[W:Multilingual education|multilingual education]] programs, the creation of the [[Academia de la Lengua Kuarupí]] in 1935, and the official recognition of Kuarupí as a national language under the [[Basic Laws of Balisca]]. The [[Indigenous Language Revitalization Program]] of 1972 expanded Kuarupí-language education, established Kuarupí-language media, and supported community-based language maintenance. Today, Kuarupí is taught in schools throughout the territories historically inhabited by the Kuarupíba, and within the [[Indigenous territories in Balisca|own territories]] regions, used in local government, and broadcast on regional radio and television.

