Primary stress is predictable, falling on the final [[syllable]] of the [[root]]. When suffixes are added, stress shifts to the final syllable of the word.

==Grammar==
{{Main|Kuarupí grammar}}

Kuarupí is an [[agglutinative language]] with extensive [[verbal morphology]]. Words are built from roots combined with multiple affixes. Nouns are marked for [[person]] (via [[possessor]] agreement) and [[number]], but not for [[grammatical case|case]]. Possession is indicated by prefixing the possessor to the possessed noun.

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Person !! Prefix !! Example !! Meaning
|-
| 1st singular || ''che-'' || ''che-sy'' || "my mother"
|-
| 2nd singular || ''nde-'' || ''nde-sy'' || "your mother"
|-
| 3rd singular || ''i-'' || ''i-sy'' || "his/her mother"
|-
| 1st plural inclusive || ''ñande-'' || ''ñande-sy'' || "our (incl.) mother"
|-
| 1st plural exclusive || ''ore-'' || ''ore-sy'' || "our (excl.) mother"
|-
| 2nd plural || ''pene-'' || ''pene-sy'' || "your (pl.) mother"
|-
| 3rd plural || ''i-...-kuéra'' || ''i-sy-kuéra'' || "their mother"
|}

Verbs are marked for [[person]], [[number]], [[tense]], [[aspect]], [[mood]], and [[evidentiality]]. The verb system features person agreement for both subject and object, tense markers including future (''-ta''), past (''ra'e-'' or ''-kue''), and unmarked present, aspect markers including progressive (''-hína''), completive (''-ma''), and habitual (''-va''), and mood markers including imperative (''-na''), conditional (''-ne''), and subjunctive (''mo-'').

Kuarupí has a rich system of [[evidentiality]] markers, indicating the source of information.

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Marker !! Evidential type !! Meaning
|-
| ''-icha'' || Direct || Speaker witnessed the event
|-
| ''-rã'' || Distant past || Speaker heard about it from others
|-
| ''-hague'' || Reportative || Speaker was told by someone
|-
| ''-ne'' || Inferential || Speaker infers from evidence
|-
| ''-mi'' || Uncertainty || Speaker is uncertain
|}

Kuarupí has [[verb–object–subject|VOS]] (verb–object–subject) as its basic word order, though other orders are possible due to the rich agreement morphology. An example sentence is ''O-ú hína ava kuárupí kokuépe'' ([oˈu ˈhi.na ˈa.βa kwa.ɾuˈpi ko.kʷeˈpe]), meaning "The Kuarupíba people are gathering in the village" (literally: "Come-are people Kuarupí village-at").

==Vocabulary==
{{Main|Kuarupí vocabulary}}

Basic vocabulary in Kuarupí includes ''ava'' ("person, people"), ''sy'' ("mother"), ''týra'' ("father"), ''oke'' ("house"), ''ka'a'' ("forest"), ''y'' ("water"), ''yva'' ("fruit"), ''ara'' ("day, sky"), ''pyhare'' ("night"), and ''tenda'' ("place"). Kuarupí has loaned extensively to the [[Baliscano|Baliscano language]].

==Writing system==
{{Main|Kuarupí alphabet}}

Kuarupí is written in the [[W:Latin script|Mesgoean script]], standardized by the [[Academia de la Lengua Kuarupí]]. The current orthography was adopted in 1974 and is used in education, media, and official documents. The alphabet consists of twenty-eight letters representing the consonant and vowel phonemes described. 

