The [[W:Glottal stop|glottal stop]] (saltillo) is represented by an apostrophe. Nasal vowels are indicated by a tilde. A traditional [[Kuarupí script]] (scriptio Kuarupí) was used historically for ceremonial purposes, but has been largely superseded by the Mesogean alphabet.

==Status and revitalization==
{{See also|Indigenous languages of Balisca}}

Kuarupí has official status as a [[Languages of Balisca|regional language of the Castejón Metropolis]] and it's surrounding metropolitan area, under Article XII of the [[Basic Laws of Balisca]]. It is used in education through bilingual education programs in historically Kuarupíba-speaking communities and taught as a subject in all schools. In media, Kuarupí-language radio and television programming is broadcast, along with newspapers and online media. In government, official documents and services are available in Kuarupí, and [[reserved seats]] exist for Kuarupíba representatives in the [[Confederal Congress]]. A growing body of contemporary Kuarupí literature exists, along with translations of world literature.

The [[Indigenous Language Revitalization Program]] has contributed to significant increases in Kuarupí speakers since 1972, with the number of speakers nearly doubling from 2.2 million to 4.2 million over five decades. Kuarupí is taught in schools throughout the [[Levante, Balisca|Levante]]. The [[Confederal University of Pindorama]] offers degree programs in Kuarupí language and literature, and trains teachers for bilingual education programs.

